course: biology
lesson: 336
title: The garden in winter: bare trees, tracks in the snow and who sleeps where
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A winter garden looks empty, but fresh snow tells on everyone: in the morning it is like a page where the night's visitors wrote their names with their feet.
explain: A neat straight line of four-toed prints with claw marks means a fox trotted through, putting its back feet into its front prints; a round print with no claw marks means the neighbour's cat; pairs of tiny prints side by side mean a sparrow hopped past.
explain: Meanwhile the hedgehog under the shed is so deeply asleep that its heart beats about twenty times a minute instead of nearly two hundred, while the squirrel is wide awake, digging up nuts and raiding the bird feeder.
explain: In cartoons winter animals snuggle in cosy burrows with scarves on. Real ones save energy in different ways: some sleep, some huddle, some eat almost all day, and a robin keeps singing «this garden is mine» even under the streetlights.
fact: Trees drop their leaves to save water when the ground is frozen, bare branches hold less snow, and the buds for next spring are already formed.
fact: A fox walks in a nearly straight line, putting its back feet into its front prints, and its prints show four toes and claw marks; dogs wander about more.
fact: A cat print shows four toes and no claw marks, because cats pull their claws in when walking, and it is rounder than a dog's print; dogs and foxes cannot pull their claws in, so their claw marks show.
fact: Small birds such as sparrows hop and leave pairs of prints, pigeons and crows walk and leave one print after another, and blackbirds can both hop and run.
fact: A hibernating hedgehog's heart slows from about 190 beats a minute to about 20, and its body cools almost to the temperature of the air; it may wake a few times in winter and move nest.
fact: Squirrels do not hibernate: they stay in their nest, called a drey, on the coldest days and eat buried nuts and seeds from feeders.
fact: When a garden pond freezes, frogs may rest in the mud at the bottom; a ball floating on the water keeps a hole open, and the ice must never be smashed with a hammer, because the shock can hurt fish and frogs.
fact: Robins defend territories all year, both males and females, and their song says «this garden is mine»; they can even sing at night under streetlights.
fact: Small birds burn a lot of energy staying warm, and a small bird can lose about a tenth of its weight in one cold night, so fat balls help, and water is as important as food in freezing weather.
fact: Ladybirds hibernate together in clusters in sheltered spots like window frames, which helps them stay warm and safe, and they come out again in spring.
exercise: Why do many trees drop their leaves before winter? | To save water when the ground is frozen | Because the leaves get too heavy with frost | Because birds eat the leaves | To let the snow reach the roots
exercise: What has a bare tree already got ready for spring? | Its buds for the new leaves and flowers | Its fruit for next year | A coat of new bark | Its autumn leaves, stored in the roots
exercise: How does a fox walk in the snow? | In a nearly straight line, back feet in its front prints | In wide zigzags, sniffing everything and leaving a messy trail | Hopping with both feet together | On its back legs only
exercise: How many toes show in a fox print? | Four, with claw marks | Five, with no claw marks | Three, like a bird | Two, like a deer
exercise: Why does a cat print show no claw marks? | Cats pull their claws in when walking | Cats have no claws on their back feet | Cat claws are too soft to leave marks | Cats walk on tiptoe with their claws up
exercise: Which animals cannot pull their claws in, so their prints show claw marks? | Dogs and foxes | Cats and lions | Only owls | Hedgehogs and rabbits
exercise: Which bird tracks come in pairs side by side? | The tracks of small hopping birds like sparrows | The tracks of big walking birds like pigeons and crows | The tracks of walking pigeons | The tracks of a swan on ice
exercise: How fast does a hibernating hedgehog's heart beat? | About 20 beats a minute | About 190 beats a minute | About 500 beats a minute | It stops completely until spring
exercise: How warm is a hibernating hedgehog's body? | It cools almost to the temperature of the air | It stays just as warm as in summer, under its thick spines | It gets warmer than in summer | It freezes solid like ice
exercise: What does a squirrel do in winter? | Stays awake, eats buried nuts and rests in its drey on the coldest days | Hibernates without waking for five months, curled up like a hedgehog in leaves | Flies south with the swallows | Sleeps under the pond ice
exercise: What is a safe way to keep a hole open in a frozen pond? | Float a ball on the water before it freezes | Pour a kettle of boiling water on the same spot every morning | Smash the ice with a hammer | Jump on the ice until it cracks
exercise: Where may frogs spend the winter in a pond? | Resting in the mud at the bottom | Frozen inside the ice | Hanging from the pond weed upside down | In the bird bath
exercise: Why does a robin sing in the middle of winter? | It defends its territory all year | It is calling for the spring to come | It is lost and calling for its mother | Robins only sing when they are cold
exercise: Who sings to defend a robin territory? | Both males and females | Only the males, never the females | Only young robins | Only females
exercise: How much weight can a small bird lose in one cold night? | About a tenth of its weight | About half its weight | None at all | About one feather's weight
exercise: Why do ladybirds gather in clusters in winter? | To stay warm and safe together | To fight over the best window | To lay eggs in the snow | To hunt the last aphids
test: «there's prints in the snow, dead straight line, little claws, 4 toes. the cat?» What is right? | Probably a fox; a cat's print shows no claw marks | Definitely the cat, cats always walk in straight lines | A pigeon | A hedgehog walking on tiptoe
test: «the pond's frozen, dad's gonna smash it with a hammer for the fish» What is better? | Do not smash it; the shock can hurt fish and frogs, a floating ball keeps a hole open | Smash it hard and fast with a hammer, then pour salt in the hole to stop it freezing again | Smash it hard and fast | Put salt on the pond
test: In a cartoon the squirrel snores in its hole all winter like a bear. What do real squirrels do? | Stay awake, resting on the coldest days and eating buried nuts | Sleep without waking until spring, just like bears and hedgehogs | Move into houses for the winter | Grow a white coat and hide in snow
test: «hedgehog's dead, it's cold as anything and barely breathing» What might be true? | It may be hibernating; its body cools and its heart slows right down | It is too hot under all those spines and needs to be put on some ice | Hedgehogs die every winter | It is just pretending for fun
test: Why does slowing down the body save energy for a hedgehog? | A slow heart and cool body burn much less fat | A slow heart makes more fat | Cold bodies need more food | It saves water, not energy
test: «why bother putting water out, it's freezing, they've got snow» What is true? | Water is as important as food when everything is frozen | Snow is better than water for birds, because it melts slowly in the beak | Birds never drink in winter | Birds can only drink warm milk
test: «stupid robin singing at 2 in the morning by the streetlamp, shut UP» Why might it sing at night? | Robins defend their patch all year and the streetlight confuses day and night | It is lost and scared of the dark, so it sings to call its mother to come and find it | It is calling the hedgehog | Robins only sing at night
test: Why would a robin need a territory in winter? | To keep a patch with enough food for itself | To keep a nest warm for eggs | To stop squirrels hibernating | To hide from swallows
test: Count them: in the snow you find tracks of 1 fox, 2 cats and 3 sparrows. How many feet made them? | 18 | 12 | 24 | 9
test: Count them: each cat paw print in the snow shows 4 toes. How many toe marks do all 4 paws of one cat leave? | 16 | 4 | 20 | 18
test: What would happen to a small bird with no food on a long freezing night? | It could lose a lot of weight and might not survive | Nothing, because small birds need no food at all in winter | It would grow fatter from the cold | It would fly to Africa at once
exam: Identify the principal reason for which deciduous trees shed their leaves in advance of winter. | The conservation of water during a period in which the ground is frozen | The provision of food for animals that hibernate | The reception of a greater quantity of snow upon the branches | The concealment of the buds from birds
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the tracks left in snow by a fox. | The tracks form a nearly straight line, each displaying four toes and the marks of claws | The tracks occur in pairs, side by side, as in the case of sparrows and other small hopping birds | The tracks display five toes and no marks of claws | The tracks are indistinguishable from those of a domestic cat
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the physiological state of a hedgehog in hibernation? | The rate of the heart is greatly reduced and the body cools nearly to the surrounding temperature | The rate of the heart increases considerably and the body grows warmer than during the summer months | The animal remains fully active beneath the snow | The body of the animal freezes solid until spring
exam: Ascertain the appropriate method for the maintenance of an opening in the ice of a garden pond. | The flotation of a ball upon the water | The repeated striking of the ice with a hammer | The pouring of boiling water onto the ice | The application of salt to the surface of the ice
